---
import Essay from '../../layouts/Essay.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import LoopVideo from '../../components/LoopVideo.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';
import survival from '../../assets/work/test2fail-survival-function-comparison.png';
import cdf from '../../assets/work/test2fail-cdf-comparison.png';
import socialCard from '../../assets/og/test2fail.png';

/* A project page: one public repository, told as the question it answers.
   It reads like an essay and shares that layout, but sits under About's
   "Selected work" (05), so the closing card leads back there. Figures come
   from the repository's own README; facts and numbers match it. */
const repo = `${SITE.github}/Test2Fail-Toolkit`;
---

<Essay
  social={{ image: socialCard, alt: 'Test2Fail Toolkit by Flora Liu: Figure 2 of the generated PDF report, three fitted life distributions over the failure histogram. floraliu.dev/work/test2fail.' }}
  docNo="05"
  title="Test2Fail Toolkit"
  description="An open-source Python tool by Flora Liu that turns test-to-failure data into a reliability report: life distribution fitting, bootstrap confidence intervals, B10, B50 and MTTF, and a PDF."
  thesis="Test-to-failure data in, a reliability report out."
  next={{
    href: '/work/piezo-motor',
    kicker: 'Next project',
    title: 'Visual servo control of a piezoelectric motor',
    promise: 'A 120 FPS camera and a PID loop steer a motor that drifts on its own.',
  }}
>
  <div class="sub">
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p class="opener">Which life model fits, how sure is the fit, and when do the first units fail?</p>
      <p>
        A test-to-failure run ends with a column of numbers. Turning it into an answer takes the
        same steps every time, so I put them in one tool.
      </p>
      <div class="proj-video">
        <LoopVideo
          src="/media/work/test2fail-workflow.mp4"
          alt="The Test2Fail GUI: picking dataset-2, entering 20,000 expected cycles, running the analysis, then scrolling through the whole PDF report."
        />
      </div>
      <p class="aside">
        <a href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/floraliu-dev/Test2Fail-Toolkit ↗</a>
        · Python · Ships with synthetic sample data only.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <h2>What it does</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <ul>
        <li>Fits Weibull, Lognormal and Exponential by maximum likelihood and picks the best one.</li>
        <li>Checks the fit with a Kolmogorov–Smirnov test.</li>
        <li>Puts 95% bootstrap confidence intervals (1,000 resamples) on the Weibull β and η.</li>
        <li>Predicts B10, B50, B95 and MTTF from 10,000 Monte Carlo samples.</li>
        <li>Suggests a maintenance interval from a target reliability and cost.</li>
        <li>Writes every plot and table into one PDF.</li>
      </ul>
      <Figure
        no="1"
        wide
        src={survival}
        alt="Survival curves for the same data under Weibull, Lognormal and Exponential fits. Their MTTFs sit within 1,000 cycles of each other, but the curves part early in life."
        caption="Three fits to one dataset. The mean lives are close; the early-life curves are not."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <h2>Why fit more than one model</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Two models can agree on the mean life and still disagree on when the first 10% fail. Field
        returns come from that early tail, so the tool reports B10 and the fit test alongside MTTF.
      </p>
      <p>
        I walk through one such dataset in <a href="/confidence">How a product earns confidence</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <h2>Does the model match the data?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        A best fit is only the best of three. The tool overlays the fitted CDF on the measured data
        and on a Monte Carlo sample drawn from the fit, then measures the largest gap between them.
      </p>
      <Figure
        no="2"
        wide
        src={cdf}
        alt="Cumulative distribution of the measured lifetimes, a simulated sample and the fitted model, lying almost on top of each other. KS statistic 0.065, p-value 0.9507."
        caption="Measured, simulated and fitted CDFs. The largest gap is 0.065 (KS test, p = 0.95): no sign the model is wrong."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <h2>Try it</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={`${repo}/releases/latest`} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
            >Download for Windows or macOS ↗</a
          >, no Python needed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/sample_report.pdf`} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
            >See a full sample report (PDF) ↗</a
          >: every plot and table in one file.
        </li>
        <li>
          Or run from source: <code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code>, then
          <code>python main.py</code>. Sample datasets are in <code>dataset/</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Essay>

<style>
  .proj-video {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .proj-video :global(video) {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 6px;
  }
</style>
